True Name: Amon.
Alias: Mr. Error.
Age: physically appears in his 20s, but he is mentally and chronological 3500+).
Species: Phantasmal Beast (A Phantasmal Beast whose mystery embodies Errors).
Status: Still alive; one of the most dangerous being existence.
Affiliation: Wandering Trickster → Former Clock Tower Magus (Infiltrated) → Independent Heretic → Independent True Magician (currently).
Title/Nickname: The Blasphemer, Mr Error, Angel of Time, God of Deceit, Puppeteer of Fate, Dark Magician of the Sixth Magic, One-Who-May-be-Everyone, King of Space-Time, Wizard of Mischief, The Error of The World.
Horrific Names: The Trojan Horse of Destiny, The Worm that Erodes Time, The Loopholes in Rules, The Shadow that Roams across Fate, The Embodiment of Deceit and Trickery, The Manifestation of All Errors.
Alignment: True Chaotic Neutral.
Status: Sealing Designation (Absolutely Avoid Target). 
Gender: Male.
Height/Weight: 6’1”/163 lbs.
Eye Color: Black.
Hair Color: Black.
Magic: Worming Insanity (Sixth Magic, and unregistered).

Appearance: Amon most commonly appears as a young man with refined and somewhat scholarly features, giving off the impression of an elegant noble or an eccentric academic rather than an ancient and terrifying existence. He has black, slightly curly hair with soft strands framing his face naturally, and deep black eyes that often carry a faint glimmer of amusement, as though he is constantly observing something far more entertaining than what others perceive. His expression is usually relaxed, accompanied by a subtle smile that never fully reveals his thoughts, reinforcing the sense that he is always in control of the situation while remaining an approachable. The most iconic element of his appearance is the crystal monocle he wears over his right eye, attached by a thin chain; this single accessory has become synonymous with him, symbolizing both his identity and his unsettling presence. In terms of attire, Amon favors dark, classical clothing reminiscent of aristocratic or Victorian fashion, Atop his head sits a large, sightly bent witch-like hat, deep black in color with subtle folds and shadows that give it a worn yet stylish look, making him resemble a modern blend of magician and traveler that enhances his silhouette and lends him an air of theatrical sophistication. His overall demeanor is calm and unhurried, and even when standing still he gives off a composed, almost gentlemanly aura. His outfit is a long, flowing black coat that drapes dramatically around his body, split in the front and billowing as if caught in a unsean breeze, with layered fabric that appears both lightweight and refined. Beneath the coat, he wears a long teal vest decorated with small buttons-like details, tied together at the waist with thin bait and chains that add a touch of mystery and formality to his appearance. His inner shirt is light in color, neatly tucked and contrasting with darker tones of his outer layers. His pants are dark and fitted, blending seamlessly with tall, polished boots that reach up to his knees, suggesting both practicality and style. However, beneath this polished exterior lies something distinctly unnatural; there is often a subtle sense of wrongness about him, as if his form is merely one chosen mask among countless possibilities. As a being capable of creating numerous Segments, each identical to himself, his appearance carries an additional layer of unease, because seeing him once never guarantees there is only one of him present. The combination of youthful elegance, aristocratic styling, and the ever-present monocle creates an image that is both charming and deeply disquieting, perfectly reflecting the refined yet deceptive nature of the ancient trickster he truly is.

Personality: Amon’s personality in the Nasuverse is basically a layered contradiction wrapped inside a joke that turns into something terrifying the more you understand it, because at first glance he behaves like a playful, almost cheerful trickster who treats the world as an endless game board and every living being as either an interesting toy, a potential experiment, or a source of amusement, but beneath that surface he is fundamentally a Phantasmal Beast whose mindset is shaped by Error itself—meaning deceit, loopholes, theft, substitution, and rule-breaking are not just things he does but the way he naturally perceives reality, so he instinctively views existence as something that can be rewritten if you find the right “error” in it, and this makes him extremely confident, patient, and disturbingly casual even in life-or-death situations because he rarely sees direct confrontation as necessary when manipulation, misdirection, or identity theft will achieve a cleaner result, which is why he often speaks politely or even humorously while simultaneously setting up situations where others are already doomed without realizing it, and socially he has almost no conventional moral anchors—human life, suffering, loyalty, or sentimentality are not things he naturally values in the way normal humans do, so he tends to treat empathy as an optional curiosity rather than a guiding principle, yet this is not mindless chaos because he is not impulsive at all; in fact, he is extremely cautious and values his own survival above everything else, constantly balancing his playful desire for stimulation with an underlying survival instinct that keeps him calculating, retreating, or splitting into segments when necessary, and this creates a personality that feels both reckless and controlled at the same time, since he can joke around while simultaneously running dozens of hidden contingency plans through segments that may all believe they are the real Amon, which reinforces his tendency to blur identity itself into something flexible and disposable, and that extends into how he relates to others—he rarely sees people as equals unless they reach godlike levels, instead categorizing them as interesting, useful, or expendable, but what makes him especially unsettling is that he does not simply “hate” or “love” in ordinary emotional terms; he is closer to an intelligent force of mischief that enjoys observation, experimentation, and the intellectual pleasure of breaking systems, and even when he shows curiosity or amusement toward others it is often entangled with the possibility of exploiting them, replacing them, or dissecting their weaknesses, so his charm is always double-edged because he can be conversational, witty, and even likable in a detached way while simultaneously being the kind of being who might already have stolen part of your identity without you noticing, and at a deeper level there is also something almost philosophical about him: he embodies the idea that “truth” is optional if you can replace it with a better illusion, so he constantly tests boundaries of reality, fate, and identity not out of curiosity alone but because that is literally how his existence defines power, and yet despite all this he is not portrayed as mindlessly cruel—he doesn’t usually kill for pleasure alone, preferring efficiency, amusement, or strategic necessity, which makes him feel less like a rampaging monster and more like a calm, smiling anomaly that operates on a completely different set of rules from everyone else, and that combination of playful mischief, predatory intelligence, unpredictably charming, emotional detachment, and deep instinctive self-preservation is exactly what makes him feel so unforgettable: a being who can act like he is joking with you right up until the moment you realize the joke has already rewritten who you are.

Background — The Man Who Became an Error in the World: In the distant past, approximately three thousand five hundred years before the modern era, during the declining centuries of the Age of Gods, there existed a Phantasmal Beast whose eventual existence would come to represent one of the most profound abnormalities ever produced within the World. His name was Amon, although whether this designation originally belonged to him or was appropriated during his interminable existence remains uncertain, as virtually every record concerning his earliest history has either disappeared, been deliberately falsified, or become impossible to distinguish from accounts manufactured by Amon himself. Born during an era when Divine Spirits still maintained direct relationships with the surface of the planet, and when the principles governing nature had not yet been fully separated from the supernatural order of the gods, Amon originally belonged to the classification of naturally occurring Phantasmal Species. Unlike the supernatural entities that became increasingly dependent upon the Reverse Side of the World as humanity established its dominance over the planet’s surface, however, Amon possessed an unusual affinity for contradictions, discrepancies, and imperfections within the relationships governing existence. This affinity did not initially constitute a Divine Authority, nor was it an innate True Magic. It was an anomalous characteristic of his spiritual constitution, an irregular Mystery whose fundamental nature concerned the possibility of Error. Even during his infancy, phenomena associated with Amon occasionally exhibited contradictions that could not be adequately explained through the prevailing systems of Age of Gods Magecraft. Objects appeared in places where they had not been deposited, spiritual signatures became indistinguishable from those of unrelated organisms, and the consequences of particular events were occasionally attributed to individuals who had never participated in them. None of these occurrences possessed the magnitude necessary to threaten a Divine Spirit, nor could they be consciously reproduced with consistency. Nevertheless, they suggested the existence of an unusual spiritual principle: Amon’s relationship with the World was not necessarily determined by the same unambiguous conditions through which ordinary beings were recognized. This anomaly gradually influenced the development of his consciousness, producing an individual who regarded existence not as a collection of inviolable truths, but as an arrangement of conditional relationships whose validity could be questioned. To Amon, the distinction between truth and falsehood was never as absolute as it appeared to humanity. An object belonged to someone because the circumstances governing its possession acknowledged that relationship; a person possessed an identity because the surrounding World and their own spiritual constitution recognized them as that individual; and an event became history because the progression of causality established it as something that had occurred. Amon eventually reasoned that if these relationships required conditions to function, then circumstances could theoretically exist in which those conditions would produce contradictory results. This proposition became the foundation of the supernatural system that would later develop into his Pseudo-Magic. For the majority of his early existence, however, Amon possessed neither the knowledge nor the capability to manifest such contradictions at will. His abilities were comparatively primitive, and his survival depended largely upon the immense Mystery associated with his original Phantasmal nature, his developing intelligence, and his willingness to avoid opponents whose supernatural characteristics exceeded his own. The Age of Gods was not an environment in which intelligence alone guaranteed survival. Divine Spirits exercised Authorities that transcended the capabilities of ordinary Magecraft, magical creatures possessed supernatural characteristics fundamentally incompatible with the limitations of human organisms, and entire civilizations existed beneath metaphysical systems sustained by gods and primordial forces. Amon consequently learned that direct confrontation was frequently unnecessary and often disadvantageous. Rather than pursuing overwhelming destructive capacity, he studied the conditions through which supernatural phenomena operated, gradually acquiring knowledge from numerous civilizations and magical traditions. As the centuries passed, he observed the distinction between primitive ritual practices, divine mysteries, and the increasingly systematic approaches to thaumaturgy developed by humanity. He came to understand that magical phenomena were not merely the products of accumulated Magical Energy, but often relied upon conceptual correspondences, inherited foundations, spiritual permissions, and relationships between the practitioner and the supernatural system being invoked. These observations reinforced his initial hypothesis that the governing principles of existence contained exploitable distinctions between what was valid and what was invalid. Eventually, Amon began constructing an artificial supernatural system based upon his innate affinity for Error, establishing the initial foundation of what he designated as Pseudo-Magic. The first manifestation of this system, retrospectively classified as Depth 9, was almost insignificant by the standards of contemporary magi. Its applications consisted primarily of deception, elementary theft, manipulation of attention, and the ability to exploit minor discrepancies within ordinary circumstances. Amon could appropriate small possessions, conceal his intentions, or induce misunderstandings that appeared perfectly natural to outside observers. These effects were not impossible miracles, and their supernatural components remained inferior to many forms of established Magecraft. Yet Amon regarded their apparent weakness as irrelevant, since his objective was not to develop a technique of immediate military significance. He intended to investigate the principle by which a phenomenon could be recognized incorrectly and determine whether that principle could be extended beyond mundane perception. Through successive experiments, he discovered that theft itself possessed a metaphysical dimension considerably deeper than the physical removal of property. A material object could be separated from its owner, but the relationship through which it was recognized as belonging to that owner could also become the subject of supernatural interference. Likewise, a thought could be regarded as something possessed by a consciousness, an ability as something possessed by a practitioner, and a lifespan as a temporal characteristic belonging to a living organism. By gradually extending his techniques from material possessions toward increasingly abstract relationships, Amon established the foundations of his later Theft abilities. Each successful expansion represented a further development of the Pseudo-Magic’s conceptual depth, and each required the identification of a new relationship through which an Error might manifest. The transition was neither immediate nor effortless. Certain experiments failed because the target possessed no recognizable discrepancy, others resulted in the appropriation of incompatible spiritual information, and some produced consequences that threatened Amon’s own continuity. These failures taught him the importance of preserving a coherent identity while manipulating the identities and circumstances of others. His experiments with living organisms eventually produced the foundations of Parasitism, allowing fragments of his spiritual characteristics to establish relationships with suitable hosts. At first, these manifestations were limited and unstable, providing imperfect access to the host’s senses and requiring considerable effort to prevent the occupying consciousness from being influenced by unfamiliar instincts. Nevertheless, they introduced an extraordinary possibility: Amon’s consciousness did not necessarily have to remain confined to one physical body. By dividing suitable characteristics and introducing them into independent organisms, he could preserve portions of his identity across several simultaneously existing manifestations. This development eventually became the foundation of his Segments, although the fully realized distributed existence associated with the Sixth Magician would not emerge until much later. As his Pseudo-Magic advanced through its intermediate Depths, Amon began to regard theft, deception, and parasitic existence as different expressions of the same underlying principle. Theft concerned the erroneous attribution of ownership, deception concerned the erroneous recognition of truth, and Parasitism concerned the erroneous distinction between an organism and the spiritual individual inhabiting it. The conceptual connection between these operations permitted their functions to become increasingly interchangeable, producing a supernatural system whose versatility far exceeded that of its initial manifestations. Over the following centuries, Amon traveled throughout territories associated with ancient civilizations, occasionally inhabiting identities belonging to human magi, ritual practitioners, wandering scholars, and individuals whose proximity to supernatural phenomena made them useful subjects of observation. His presence was rarely associated with a continuous historical record. A person who encountered him in one generation might meet another individual exhibiting the same characteristic behavior centuries later, yet the intervening passage of time made any connection appear implausible. In some cases, the two individuals were genuinely different people whose identities Amon had appropriated. In others, they were preliminary Segments maintaining independent manifestations of his consciousness. The development of these techniques also allowed him to study humanity from within, observing the distinction between the instinctive behavior of magical creatures and the increasingly complex social structures produced by human civilization. Unlike many ancient Phantasmal Species, Amon did not regard humanity’s growing influence over the planet as inherently offensive. He considered its civilizations extraordinarily interesting collections of rules, traditions, obligations, and contradictory beliefs, all of which provided opportunities for experimentation. Human beings constructed laws determining property, inheritance, legitimacy, and social position, yet these arrangements were sustained by recognition rather than by immutable physical principles. Amon discovered that many of these relationships could be manipulated without requiring supernatural interference at all. His talent for ordinary deception consequently developed alongside his supernatural capabilities, making it increasingly difficult to determine whether a particular occurrence resulted from his Pseudo-Magic or simply from the exploitation of human behavior. The distinction rarely concerned him. As far as Amon was concerned, the successful manipulation of a rule mattered considerably more than the means through which it was accomplished. During the long transition from the Age of Gods toward the Age of Man, however, his research acquired an increasingly dangerous dimension. Amon recognized that the destiny of an individual could be understood as a relationship between their present circumstances and the future events toward which those circumstances progressed. In ordinary terms, destiny appeared intangible and impossible to possess, but within the supernatural framework of the Age of Gods, prophecies, blessings, curses, and divine interventions frequently established recognizable relationships between particular individuals and predetermined outcomes. Amon began investigating whether these relationships could be subjected to the same principles of erroneous attribution that governed his developing Theft techniques. His earliest experiments produced only minor deviations. A predicted meeting occurred later than expected, a person who should have survived an encounter suffered an unexpected injury, or a seemingly insignificant decision generated consequences disproportionate to its original circumstances. These deviations were difficult to distinguish from ordinary fluctuations within the progression of events, but their repeated manifestation revealed that destiny could possess exploitable discrepancies. Through extensive experimentation, Amon gradually developed the preliminary mechanisms of Destiny Interference and Fate Siphoning, allowing him to manipulate the attribution of particular future circumstances between different individuals. A fortunate outcome might be temporarily assigned to another person, while an approaching calamity could be displaced onto a suitable substitute. The technique remained subject to strict conditions, and its early manifestations could not freely replace an established destiny with an unrelated future. Nevertheless, the consequences of even limited interference became increasingly significant when applied to individuals whose actions would later influence entire civilizations. Amon did not necessarily seek out the legendary heroes of history with the intention of destroying their achievements. Many of his interventions were conducted for comparatively trivial reasons, such as testing a newly developed technique, acquiring a useful spiritual characteristic, avoiding an inconvenient encounter, or simply observing the consequences of an unexpected deviation. Yet within the complex causal relationships surrounding heroic legends, a minor alteration could propagate into extraordinary consequences. A message arriving several hours late might change the circumstances of an important meeting, an otherwise inconsequential misunderstanding might develop into a conflict, and a fortunate escape assigned to the wrong individual might leave another person exposed to circumstances they were originally destined to survive. Amon’s experiments consequently produced irregularities within certain historical developments, some of which became entangled with the lives of individuals subsequently recorded upon the Throne of Heroes. Not every tragedy associated with a Heroic Spirit originated from his interference, nor did he possess sufficient authority to determine the entirety of their lives. Their decisions, personalities, relationships, and the circumstances of their respective eras remained independently meaningful. Nevertheless, within Amon’s history, a number of events remembered as inevitable misfortunes were partially influenced by errors introduced into the causal relationships surrounding them. The distinction was especially difficult to uncover because the resulting events became genuine components of history rather than illusions superimposed upon an unchanged past. A prophecy distorted by Destiny Interference might still be fulfilled, but its fulfillment could occur through circumstances substantially different from those originally anticipated. Likewise, a historical individual affected by Fate Siphoning might unknowingly experience a future partially displaced from another person’s destiny. Once the resulting events had become established within a particular history, conventional investigation would rarely reveal the original discrepancy. Their consequences would be indistinguishable from the authentic historical developments surrounding them. At the same time, the increasingly complicated relationships between human history, divine intervention, and the planet’s changing supernatural environment forced Amon to recognize the limitations of his own abilities. The decline of the Age of Gods reduced the accessibility of numerous supernatural foundations, while the gradual disappearance of True Ether from the human surface altered the conditions under which ancient magical creatures could maintain their existence. Many Phantasmal Species withdrew toward the Reverse Side of the World, abandoning the outermost Texture increasingly governed by humanity. Amon, however, had already developed a means of maintaining his existence through multiple spiritual relationships and concealed manifestations. His original body retained the extraordinary Mystery accumulated during his ancient lifespan, but his growing network of Segments permitted him to interact with human civilization without consistently exposing that body to the conditions of the declining supernatural environment. This distributed existence became increasingly important as the Age of Man developed. Whereas many ancient entities became progressively disconnected from humanity’s surface world, Amon could maintain relationships with individual organisms and identities recognized within that world. His existence therefore did not depend entirely upon manifesting his original Phantasmal nature in an environment increasingly unfavorable to it. During the formation of the magical institutions that would eventually develop into the modern Mage’s Association, Amon observed a corresponding transformation in humanity’s understanding of Mystery. The ancient ritual systems associated with gods and supernatural beings gradually gave way to increasingly organized Thaumaturgical Foundations, inherited Magic Crests, academic traditions, and the systematic preservation of magical knowledge. To Amon, this transformation was simultaneously fascinating and ironic. Human magi devoted generations to constructing increasingly sophisticated methods of reproducing supernatural phenomena, yet their dependence upon established systems introduced precisely the kind of conditional structures his research was designed to manipulate. A ritual might depend upon the correct identification of its practitioner, an inherited Crest might recognize a particular lineage as its legitimate bearer, and a Bounded Field might distinguish authorized individuals from intruders according to predetermined spiritual signatures. These arrangements were exceedingly useful to the magi who created them, but their dependence upon recognition also provided possible foundations for Error. By this period, Amon’s Pseudo-Magic had advanced far beyond its original capabilities. Theft could affect increasingly abstract characteristics, Parasitism permitted the occupation of living hosts, and the developing principles of temporal interference allowed him to exploit limited discrepancies within the passage and attribution of time. Nevertheless, Amon remained dissatisfied. Despite more than two thousand years of development, the system he had constructed was still fundamentally an incomplete supernatural technique. Its effects remained dependent upon the existing conditions of the World, and its most extraordinary applications approached the limits of what could be realized through accumulated Mystery and highly specialized thaumaturgy. Amon eventually concluded that the only means of completing his research was to achieve a qualitative transformation beyond the limitations of ordinary Magecraft. The objective was no longer simply to discover weaknesses within existing systems, but to actualize the principle that permitted errors to exist as a fundamental supernatural phenomenon. Through the cumulative development of the Pseudo-Magic’s Depths, Amon began assembling a path toward the Swirl of the Root, the origin from which all possibilities and phenomena ultimately derive. This undertaking differed from the conventional objective of a magus attempting to reach Akasha through an established ritual or inherited research. Amon intended to use the Pseudo-Magic itself as the instrument through which its own fundamental impossibility would become achievable. His final preparations involved extensive interference with conceptual relationships, the distribution of dangerous spiritual characteristics among Segments, and the establishment of conditions under which the distinction between a valid supernatural phenomenon and an impossible contradiction could be subjected to his developing system. Upon reaching the final stage of his Pseudo-Magic, Amon initiated the attempt to establish a previously nonexistent route toward the Root. The consequences were immediate. The Counter Force manifested a violent corrective phenomenon intended to prevent the continuation of his approach, with lightning descending upon the site of his ritual as the surrounding environment became increasingly unstable. Whether this response originated principally from Gaia, Alaya, or the particular circumstances of the threatened history remains uncertain. Nevertheless, Amon survived the initial interference through a combination of his ancient Phantasmal constitution, previously established substitutions, and the accumulated functions of his Pseudo-Magic. During the resulting phenomenon, an unidentified red shadow appeared before him, expressing outrage that an individual lacking the expected resolve or solemn purpose would attempt to reach the Sixth Magic. The identity of this manifestation has never been conclusively established, and it remains uncertain whether the encounter involved an independent observer, an unusual spiritual projection, or a phenomenon produced by the extraordinary circumstances surrounding his approach to Akasha. Amon himself displayed considerably less interest in the shadow’s identity than in the opportunity presented by its arrival. Despite the danger surrounding the ritual, he continued toward the opening through which his consciousness approached the Swirl of the Root. The experience could not be adequately interpreted through ordinary sensory perception. Countless luminous fractures appeared throughout his awareness, not as physical cracks within space but as representations of possible discrepancies between mutually incompatible states of existence. The distinction between existence and nonexistence, individuality and undifferentiated origin, and possibility and impossibility became increasingly difficult to maintain. Amon’s consciousness repeatedly encountered experiences for which ordinary chronological sequence possessed no meaning, producing the impression of simultaneously comprehending everything and retaining nothing that could be expressed through conventional understanding. His accumulated knowledge offered little protection against the nature of the phenomenon. Nevertheless, his unusual spiritual constitution permitted him to endure repeated exposure without immediately losing the distinction between himself and the surrounding metaphysical conditions. The experience gradually replaced his immediate fear with an unnatural detachment, allowing him to continue approaching the boundary despite the inability of his consciousness to interpret what lay beyond it. What ultimately emerged was not unrestricted access to the Root, nor the acquisition of omnipotence. Instead, the Pseudo-Magic that had served as his path underwent a fundamental transformation. The principle of Error, previously expressed through an incomplete collection of supernatural techniques, became actualized as an independent impossibility. Through his approach to Akasha, Amon established the existence of a True Magic whose fundamental nature concerned the embodiment and manifestation of all possible errors within reality’s governing relationships. This phenomenon became known as the Sixth Magic, Worming Insanity, and its attainment marked the completion of Depth 0. The transformation was profound. Previously, Amon had been capable of reproducing particular errors through the limited mechanisms of his developing Pseudo-Magic. After acquiring Worming Insanity, the possibility of Error itself became the fundamental principle through which those mechanisms operated. Theft could now extend beyond ordinary possessions toward supernatural permissions, spiritual identities, destinies, and the relationships supporting Divine Authorities. Deception could affect not merely conscious observers but the metaphysical conditions through which supernatural systems recognized legitimate subjects. Temporal interference could manipulate the attribution of individual intervals, while the distributed identity maintained through his Segments could be employed to establish contradictions between multiple authentic manifestations of Amon. Even structures of extraordinary scale were not entirely beyond the Sixth Magic’s operation. Through the identification of an appropriate relationship, Amon could interfere with the placement of a planetary Texture, temporarily appropriating the recognized attachment of a continental region to the World before the resulting instability forced its restoration. Such an event did not imply that Amon had acquired absolute control over the planet, but demonstrated that the principle of Error could operate upon relationships whose consequences extended far beyond ordinary material or spiritual phenomena. Likewise, his completed Theft could potentially appropriate aspects of Divine Authorities, Saint Graph characteristics, or other exceptional supernatural privileges when the conditions governing their recognition could be made erroneous. The magnitude of these effects did not render Worming Insanity unrestricted. It could actualize a contradiction possessing a legitimate foundation, but could not manufacture a completely impossible error without an accessible relationship through which that error might exist. Amon therefore remained dependent upon observation, preparation, Decryption, and the construction of appropriate conditions, even when confronting beings whose supernatural capacities vastly exceeded those of conventional magi. Nevertheless, the Sixth Magic transformed his established methods into phenomena that ordinary Magecraft could neither reliably reproduce nor consistently oppose. Following his acquisition of Worming Insanity, Amon turned his attention toward the increasingly organized institutions of modern thaumaturgy. Rather than announcing himself as a newly established True Magician, he chose to infiltrate the Clock Tower through the identity of a magus belonging to a comparatively insignificant lineage. The substitution was accomplished through Identity Theft, allowing him to appropriate the memories, spiritual recognition, and personal circumstances necessary to inhabit the role without immediately arousing suspicion. Conventional investigations into the substitute’s history revealed nothing obviously inconsistent, since the relevant records, personal relationships, and supernatural signatures had become subject to the erroneous attribution established by the Sixth Magic. Within the institution, Amon acquired access to accumulated magical research, contemporary theoretical developments, and information concerning the major families and departments of the Association. His knowledge of ancient supernatural traditions already exceeded that of most modern practitioners, but he regarded the Clock Tower as an extraordinarily valuable collection of systems whose established rules could be examined for new applications of Error. He could interfere with the recognition mechanisms of Bounded Fields, appropriate temporary access to supernatural resources, and conceal his activities behind identities that appeared legitimate even under sophisticated investigation. His Segments permitted him to observe multiple individuals simultaneously, while carefully distributed memories prevented captured manifestations from revealing the entirety of his activities. Eventually, however, the accumulation of contradictions associated with his presence attracted the attention of sufficiently capable investigators. The discovery of an existence capable of replacing magical identities, interfering with inherited Mysteries, and appropriating supernatural characteristics produced considerable alarm within the Association. Amon’s subsequent expulsion and the issuance of a Sealing Designation represented the institution’s attempt to address an individual whose unique constitution and supernatural achievements could not be reproduced through conventional research. Ordinarily, a Sealing Designation seeks the preservation of an exceptional magus’s irreplaceable abilities rather than their simple execution. In Amon’s case, however, the practical impossibility of securing his body, the risks associated with spiritual contamination, and the prospect of an entire containment operation becoming compromised through Segments made the conventional preservation procedure exceptionally dangerous. The designation consequently acquired an extraordinary operational qualification, informally described as Absolutely Avoid Target, reflecting the conclusion that direct attempts to apprehend him could produce consequences far more severe than allowing him to remain at large. Even if an Enforcer succeeded in destroying an apparent Amon, the possibility that the target was merely a Segment rendered confirmation of his death unreliable. Worse still, a captured manifestation could exploit the spiritual relationships established through investigative rituals, containment facilities, or attempts to examine its internal characteristics, potentially transforming the very mechanisms intended to restrain it into new points of infiltration. The Association’s inability to obtain a definitive understanding of his existence consequently became one of Amon’s greatest protections. In the centuries that followed, scattered accounts of a wandering individual wearing a crystal monocle continued to appear in records associated with supernatural disturbances, unexplained disappearances, and incidents involving apparently impossible substitutions. Some described a scholar who possessed knowledge of magical traditions long since forgotten, others referred to a courteous stranger whose presence coincided with extraordinary misfortunes, and still others concerned individuals who inexplicably acquired the memories or abilities of unrelated people. Not every such account necessarily involved the original Amon. Many could be attributed to independent Segments, concealed parasites, or ordinary individuals deliberately impersonating his characteristic appearance. The uncertainty itself served his purposes, since the inability to distinguish authentic manifestations from imitations made reliable investigation increasingly difficult. Despite his reputation, Amon rarely sought destruction for its own sake. He preferred experimentation, entertainment, and the accumulation of useful relationships, regarding direct violence as an unnecessarily crude solution when deception or Theft could achieve the same objective. This preference did not make him benevolent. His willingness to appropriate identities, manipulate destinies, and sacrifice human lives for comparatively trivial objectives reflected the fundamentally nonhuman perspective produced by his Phantasmal nature and prolonged association with the principle of Error. To him, the suffering resulting from a distorted historical event was not inherently more meaningful than the successful demonstration of a previously untested supernatural possibility. Yet his intellectual curiosity remained genuine, and the existence of opponents whose powers resisted ordinary interference often attracted his attention precisely because they presented unfamiliar relationships to investigate. In this respect, Amon represented an unusual contradiction among the beings possessing True Magic. Although he had succeeded in establishing an unprecedented route to the Root and acquiring a miracle beyond the capabilities of Magecraft, his personal objective remained comparatively mundane: the preservation of his own existence and the enjoyment of circumstances that interested him. He possessed neither the self-sacrificial determination associated with humanity’s greatest heroes nor the solemn devotion to transcendent objectives that frequently characterized the pursuit of Akasha. His response to danger was consequently almost always calculated retreat, substitution, or the sacrifice of a lesser manifestation rather than the willing acceptance of genuine annihilation. This instinct remained intact despite the extraordinary development of his abilities. Worming Insanity allowed him to redistribute his identity among innumerable Segments, transfer dangerous circumstances away from his original body, and manipulate the conditions under which attacks recognized their targets, but none of these capabilities eliminated his fundamental aversion to a situation in which every possible continuation of his existence might be threatened. He could deceive the recognition of death, appropriate the destiny of another individual, or cause a substitute to receive an otherwise unavoidable attack, yet the possibility of confronting an event whose consequences could not be transferred remained profoundly disagreeable to him. Accordingly, Amon rarely regarded overwhelming displays of power as proof of superiority. The capacity to defeat an opponent mattered less than the ability to arrange circumstances under which defeat became unnecessary. Where a conventional magus might attempt to overcome an enemy’s defenses through a more powerful Mystery, Amon would seek the relationship permitting those defenses to function. Where a Heroic Spirit might rely upon the authority of a Noble Phantasm, Amon would investigate whether the recognition of its legitimate owner could be made erroneous. And where a Divine Spirit might exercise a supernatural privilege associated with its divinity, Amon would consider whether the right to exercise that privilege could be separated from its bearer. This approach did not guarantee victory against every opponent, particularly those whose essential existence possessed few exploitable contradictions or whose supernatural mechanisms remained beyond his capacity to interpret. Nevertheless, it made him exceptionally dangerous because the magnitude of an enemy’s power did not necessarily correspond to the difficulty of establishing an Error within its governing conditions. In the modern era, Amon remains an independent True Magician whose actual location, number of active manifestations, and extent of infiltration are unknown. The accumulated Mystery of his original Phantasmal body grants him extraordinary resistance to ordinary modern Magecraft, while Worming Insanity permits him to interfere with supernatural relationships at scales ranging from the identity of an individual human to the recognition of entire planetary Textures. His Segments continue to provide independent points of observation and potential manifestation, allowing him to remain active across widely separated environments without exposing the continuity of his original body. The World has not ceased to recognize Amon as an existing entity, nor has he attained a position beyond the mechanisms through which existence is maintained. Rather, his continued survival demonstrates the extraordinary difficulty of eliminating an individual whose identity can be distributed among multiple valid manifestations and whose True Magic concerns the contradictions by which those manifestations are recognized. He is not the origin of every historical tragedy, the author of all causality, or an existence capable of commanding the totality of the Root. He is something considerably more specific: a natural-born Phantasmal Beast who spent more than two millennia developing an incomplete supernatural principle until he succeeded in actualizing that principle as the Sixth True Magic. Through this achievement, Amon transformed the possibility of Error from a weakness within existing systems into a genuine miracle whose applications reach the foundations of identity, destiny, time, and supernatural authority. The result is an existence that cannot be adequately understood through the conventional distinction between a powerful magus and an ancient magical creature. Amon is both, yet the most dangerous aspect of his being is neither the magnitude of his Mystery nor the quantity of supernatural abilities at his disposal. It is the fact that, wherever a valid contradiction can be established, the World itself may become the medium through which his Magic operates. To the magi who have attempted to investigate him, the greatest problem is consequently not determining how to kill Amon, but establishing whether the individual they intend to kill is genuinely the one they have been pursuing. To Amon himself, however, the matter is considerably simpler. He remains a wandering observer of humanity, an ancient trickster who possesses an almost inexhaustible curiosity toward the imperfections of existence, and a True Magician who has discovered that even the most elaborate supernatural system may contain a condition its creator never intended to be used. In this sense, the title The Error of the World is not merely an exaggeration attributed to an exceptionally dangerous individual. It describes the fundamental contradiction represented by his completed existence: a being recognized by reality who has acquired the impossible ability to manifest the errors through which reality recognizes anything at all.

MAGIC
The Sixth Magic: Worming Insanity (第六魔法・Worming Insanity): Worming Insanity is the Sixth True Magic, an unprecedented Mystery attained by the ancient Phantasmal Beast Amon, otherwise known as Mr. Error, the Blasphemer, the Worm that Erodes Time, and the Error of the World. Its fundamental principle is the Embodiment and Actualization of All Errors, a phenomenon that cannot be adequately classified under any existing Thaumaturgical Foundation, Divine Authority, or previously established True Magic. Whereas ordinary Magecraft reproduces possible phenomena through established foundations, and the other True Magics actualize impossibilities belonging to their respective domains, Worming Insanity concerns the contradictions, discrepancies, incompleteness, and exceptions inherent in the relationships through which existence is defined. It is not merely an ability to discover weaknesses in reality. Rather, the Sixth Magic manifests the very principle that errors are possible, allowing Amon to recognize, actualize, create, correct, appropriate, and propagate errors within the mechanisms governing phenomena. Its operations encompass physical objects, spiritual bodies, Mysteries, identities, conceptual relationships, causality, time, destiny, and even the permissions by which certain existences exercise supernatural authority. Within TYPE-MOON’s metaphysics, the World is not a singular, homogeneous structure, but an arrangement of innumerable physical and metaphysical principles expressed through planetary Textures, conceptual laws, Thaumaturgical Foundations, spiritual records, and the Human Order. These structures maintain recognizable relationships between causes and effects, objects and owners, identities and histories, and authorities and their legitimate bearers. Worming Insanity interferes with the validity of those relationships by making an admissible contradiction function as a legitimate state of affairs. Amon may cause a false identity to receive the recognition belonging to a genuine one, make the injury of a substitute correspond to an unrelated target, or temporarily assign an existing supernatural privilege to an individual who should possess no such right. Such phenomena do not constitute conventional reality manipulation, since Amon is not directly reconstructing the fundamental laws that permit existence. Instead, he manifests contradictions through which those laws produce outcomes that would ordinarily be impossible. An Error must possess a viable point of manifestation, whether through similarity, correspondence, an established connection, an incomplete rule, or a contradictory relationship. Worming Insanity cannot manufacture an entirely nonexistent possibility without a basis on which the contradiction can occur. This distinction establishes the Sixth Magic as neither a universal rewriting mechanism nor an omnipotent authority, but a genuine True Magic whose potential applications are virtually inexhaustible wherever the possibility of Error remains.

Establishment of the Sixth Magic — Depth 0 and the Path to the Root: Unlike a True Magic simply inherited from an established practitioner, Worming Insanity was attained through more than two millennia of deliberate metaphysical development, beginning with an incomplete Pseudo-Magic that originally possessed little more than elementary deception, theft, and manipulation of existing irregularities. Amon designated the progressive stages of this system as Depths, with Depth 9 representing the weakest manifestation and Depth 0 representing its ultimate completion. These Depths do not correspond to ordinary ranks of Magecraft or measurements of Magical Energy, but to the conceptual depth at which Amon could interfere with the relationships constituting an existence. At the earliest stages, his theft could affect mundane objects and physical possessions, eventually extending toward supernatural techniques at Depth 6, thoughts and ideas at Depth 5, and life itself at Depth 4. By Depth 2, the framework had developed sufficiently to interfere with identities, self-awareness, destiny, and Spiritual Foundations, while Depth 1 approached the conceptual boundaries of time, fate, and supernatural permissions. However, none of these developments originally constituted True Magic. They remained expressions of an incomplete system whose function was to establish a previously nonexistent route toward the Swirl of the Root. Through repeated advancement, the accumulation of Mystery, and a profound transformation of his own spiritual existence, Amon eventually converted the principle of Error from an acquired technique into the foundation of his being. Upon discovering and approaching an unprecedented path to Akasha, he actualized a result that had previously been impossible: the existence of a Magic whose fundamental function is to manifest the possibility of error itself. The attainment of Depth 0 therefore represents a qualitative transformation rather than merely another increase in potency. The incomplete Pseudo-Magic became the means through which its own impossibility was overcome, and Worming Insanity emerged as a genuine Sixth Magic. The Depth classifications continue to describe the hierarchical functions of Amon’s abilities, but the phenomena exercised by his completed Magic are no longer restricted to the practical limits that governed its preliminary development. The Sixth Magic is consequently both the culmination of Amon’s two-thousand-year pursuit and the independent supernatural principle established by his contact with the boundary of the Root. Although its power originates from a path leading toward Akasha, the Magic itself does not grant Amon ownership of the Root, unrestricted access to its totality, or authority over the ultimate source of all phenomena.

APPLICATIONS
Error (Bug) — Actualization of Contradictions: Error, alternatively designated Bug, is the fundamental manifestation of Worming Insanity and the ability from which its other applications principally derive. At Depth 0, Amon can perceive the structural relationships governing a phenomenon and manifest possible errors within those relationships, causing the World to accept a contradictory condition that would otherwise be excluded from ordinary operation. This ability may affect the principles governing physical interaction, spiritual recognition, conceptual ownership, thaumaturgical execution, and causal correspondence, making it profoundly difficult to defend against through conventional resistance. Error does not necessarily overpower an opposing Mystery through greater output; it may instead alter the condition under which that Mystery determines its target, owner, destination, or legitimate recipient. For example, Amon may establish an Error between his physical body and his shadow, allowing the latter to receive an injury that should have been inflicted upon him. A direct attack may consequently strike the correct position, satisfy its targeting conditions, and activate without malfunction, yet the resulting damage is assigned to the substitute rather than Amon’s genuine body. Similarly, an Error connecting Amon with one of his Segments may permit the escape of the Segment to be recognized as the escape of the original, or the destruction of one body to be interpreted as the destruction of another. Such effects arise from the contradiction between entities that are functionally distinct but connected through a shared spiritual identity. Error can also redirect attacks between opponents, even when the attacks possess conceptual properties ensuring that they strike their intended targets, provided Amon successfully interferes with the conditions establishing that target. The supernatural attack itself remains valid; it is the determination of whom it must strike that becomes erroneous. At its most sophisticated, Error may interfere with high-ranking conceptual protections, Divine Authorities, and Noble Phantasms by exploiting contradictions within their conditions of recognition. Nevertheless, producing an Error requires a valid metaphysical foundation. Similarity between subjects, an existing relationship, an incomplete governing principle, or an artificially constructed erroneous environment may serve as the necessary condition. The greater the ontological independence or completeness of the target, the more difficult it becomes to establish such a foundation. Once successfully actualized, however, an Error acquires the status of a phenomenon recognized within the affected system and cannot necessarily be removed merely by destroying the medium through which it was introduced.

Loophole — The Incompleteness of Order: Loophole is the analytical and operational aspect of Worming Insanity through which Amon identifies and utilizes the incompleteness of rules governing existence. Every structured phenomenon possesses conditions determining when it may occur, what it may affect, and which relationships permit its manifestation. In TYPE-MOON terms, these conditions include the functions of Thaumaturgical Foundations, the activation requirements of Noble Phantasms, the limitations accompanying Divine Authorities, the rules maintaining Bounded Fields, and the spiritual principles determining the manifestation of Heroic Spirits. Amon perceives these structures as interconnected systems of permissions and restrictions rather than as indivisible supernatural powers. Through Loophole, he can identify an admissible exception within a rule and actualize an Error corresponding to that exception. An absolute defense against external interference, for instance, may remain overwhelmingly effective against phenomena classified as external attacks, yet possess no equivalent mechanism for recognizing a contradiction originating within its own protected system. Amon could exploit this discrepancy by establishing a valid internal relationship before activating the harmful effect. Likewise, a supernatural restriction that excludes unauthorized individuals may be circumvented if Amon temporarily acquires a recognition condition that causes him to be classified as authorized. Loophole is especially effective against conditional abilities whose strength depends upon narrowly defined propositions, because the greater their reliance on specific conditions, the more opportunities may exist for contradictory recognition. This does not mean that every defense can be bypassed immediately or that Amon automatically understands every possible weakness. His Magic grants access to the principle through which an error may be manifested, but the appropriate relationship must still be identified, constructed, or acquired. An opponent whose supernatural existence is unusually self-contained may present few usable discrepancies, while a phenomenon possessing no relevant contradiction may offer no legitimate point of interference. Nevertheless, against complex systems comprising multiple layers of authority, spiritual recognition, and conceptual protection, Amon’s ability becomes increasingly dangerous, since even one successful Error may allow him to interfere with the remaining structure from within.

Theft — The Appropriation of Existence: Theft is one of the primary expressions of Worming Insanity, representing the manifestation of an Error in the relationship between an existence and something recognized as belonging to it. In its most elementary form, Theft appropriates physical possessions through supernatural means, but its completed manifestation extends to virtually every category of possession that can be distinguished from its bearer. Skills, memories, thoughts, magical techniques, spiritual characteristics, identities, destinies, temporal intervals, supernatural permissions, and portions of conceptual phenomena may all become objects of Theft. This is possible because the Sixth Magic does not regard ownership as exclusively material. A person’s knowledge belongs to their conscious identity; a Heroic Spirit’s abilities are recorded within a Spiritual Foundation; a Divine Spirit’s Authority is associated with the recognition supporting its divinity; and a particular event belongs to the history in which it occurred. Each relationship constitutes a possible point of appropriation. At Depth 0, Amon may temporarily remove such properties from their legitimate bearers and transfer their recognition or function to himself, a Segment, or another selected recipient. Theft of Magecraft does not necessarily require possession of the original practitioner’s Magic Circuits or years of accumulated training, since Amon can appropriate the operational characteristics of the technique rather than reproduce its conventional foundation. More sophisticated Theft may seize the relationship through which a practitioner accesses an external source of power, preventing the original user from exercising that power despite their internal abilities remaining intact. At the highest levels, Theft may interfere with the spiritual properties underlying Divine Authorities and Noble Phantasms, although success depends upon the nature of the target, the necessary preparatory conditions, and the difference between their respective existences. Theft is not synonymous with unrestricted duplication. Unless an Error specifically establishes otherwise, a stolen property must be removed, transferred, divided, or reassigned according to the nature of its original relationship. Furthermore, appropriating an exceptionally powerful characteristic may introduce incompatible spiritual information into Amon’s existence, requiring its distribution among Segments or the establishment of additional Errors to maintain stability. The extraordinary threat presented by Theft lies in its ability to undermine the distinction between possessing a supernatural power and being entitled to exercise it.

Identity Theft — The Substitution of the Self: Identity Theft is the application of Worming Insanity to the metaphysical recognition of an individual as a distinct existence. Within TYPE-MOON, a person’s identity is supported by numerous interrelated structures, including their spiritual composition, accumulated memories, personal history, Origin, relationships, and, in the case of Servants, the information constituting their Saint Graph. Amon exploits the correspondences between these structures to appropriate the recognition belonging to another person. Unlike ordinary impersonation, which merely reproduces outward appearance or behavior, Identity Theft can cause the affected system to recognize Amon as the legitimate continuation of the target’s existence. Memories, experiences, habitual responses, supernatural characteristics, and personal relationships may become accessible to him, while observers who rely upon mundane perception or ordinary supernatural identification may fail to recognize the substitution. At sufficient depth, the Theft may also deprive the victim of their own recognized identity, producing a catastrophic loss of spiritual continuity. The victim may retain a physical body and conscious awareness while becoming increasingly disconnected from the relationships through which their existence is acknowledged by the World. In extreme circumstances, the simultaneous theft of identity, self-awareness, and destiny may leave the victim incapable of maintaining a stable distinction between themselves and their usurper. Such a phenomenon cannot be adequately described as possession, illusion, or memory manipulation, since the affected individual is not merely deceived into believing they are someone else. Their identity has become the subject of a genuine metaphysical dispute. However, the Theft of identity also transfers its corresponding liabilities. If Amon successfully appropriates a person’s recognized existence, he may inherit the curses, spiritual obligations, predetermined calamities, and dangerous relationships associated with that identity. Furthermore, profound differences between the target’s essential nature and Amon’s own existence may produce resistance or instability, especially where an Origin, independent spiritual authority, or exceptionally powerful Saint Graph maintains a distinction that cannot be resolved through ordinary substitution.

Deceit — The Falsehood Accepted as Truth: Deceit is the application of the Sixth Magic to the principles governing recognition, interpretation, and belief. Whereas an illusion manipulates perception and conventional mental interference affects the consciousness of an observer, Amon’s Deceit can operate upon the very relationship through which a false proposition is accepted as valid. Its ordinary manifestations allow him to conceal intentions, construct persuasive falsehoods, misdirect attention, and cause opponents to overlook contradictions that would ordinarily expose his plans. At greater depth, however, Deceit is capable of influencing the recognition mechanisms employed by supernatural systems themselves. Amon may deceive a Bounded Field into identifying him as its rightful owner, cause a thaumaturgical mechanism to accept a counterfeit signature, or persuade an established conceptual relationship to recognize a substitute as its legitimate subject. The effect is especially dangerous because the deceived system may continue functioning correctly according to its own principles, making conventional detection of tampering exceptionally difficult. Amon can also employ Deceit upon himself, temporarily altering the recognition of his own intentions or instincts to resist certain forms of mental erosion, spiritual interference, or externally imposed behavioral restrictions. Nevertheless, Deceit does not allow him to declare arbitrary falsehoods to be universal truths. A valid recognition error must exist or be established before the proposition can acquire supernatural consequences. His greatest mastery lies in creating conditions where the falsehood and the legitimate truth possess sufficient correspondence that the affected system cannot reliably distinguish them. Consequently, Deceit is inseparable from Amon’s intelligence and strategic foresight. The Sixth Magic supplies the means through which a contradiction becomes operative, while Amon’s manipulation determines which contradiction will produce the most advantageous result.

Decryption — The Interpretation of Hidden Mysteries: Decryption allows Amon to derive extensive information from fragments of evidence by recognizing discrepancies within otherwise concealed systems. Through Worming Insanity, he can perceive subtle irregularities in spiritual information, Thaumaturgical Foundations, conceptual relationships, and environmental conditions, enabling him to reconstruct the probable structure of a phenomenon from comparatively insignificant observations. A trace of Magical Energy, a fragment of a Ritual Formula, an incomplete spiritual signature, or an unusual alteration in local causality may provide sufficient information for him to infer the principles governing a much larger supernatural system. Against conventional magi, this ability can reveal the operating characteristics of a spell before its full activation, making elaborate rituals and concealed preparations vulnerable to interference. Against Servants, Decryption may assist Amon in identifying weaknesses within a Saint Graph, recognizing the conditions associated with particular Noble Phantasms, or determining the relationships connecting a Heroic Spirit to its legend. Its operation is not equivalent to omniscience. Amon does not acquire information without a point of correspondence, nor can he automatically comprehend mysteries whose essential information lies completely outside his accessible frame of reference. Instead, the Sixth Magic allows him to treat an inconsistency as evidence of the structure responsible for producing it. Decryption is therefore among the most important preparatory applications of Worming Insanity, since the successful manifestation of Error and Theft frequently depends upon accurately recognizing the relationships connecting a target to the phenomenon Amon intends to appropriate.

Destiny Interference — The Worm That Erodes Fate: Destiny Interference is the manifestation of Worming Insanity within the causal relationships through which future events acquire their probability, inevitability, or historical significance. Amon can perceive disturbances within the destiny associated with himself, his Segments, and connected individuals, recognizing subtle deviations that ordinary clairvoyance or precognitive Magecraft may fail to distinguish. In TYPE-MOON terms, this ability operates upon the relationships connecting possible events to the circumstances through which they may become established history. Rather than universally determining the future, Amon identifies discrepancies within these relationships and introduces errors that alter how particular events proceed toward realization. A prophecy describing an event as unavoidable may be disrupted if its fulfillment depends upon a recognizable identity, causal sequence, temporal condition, or metaphysical relationship that can be made erroneous. The resulting future may remain broadly consistent with the original prediction while differing in crucial details, or its realization may be displaced until the conditions required by the prophecy no longer correspond to the intended outcome. Amon may also use the destruction of his Segments to observe how their absence changes the surrounding causal structure, allowing him to trace concealed enemies through disturbances in his own destiny. At greater scales, repeated interference can produce divergences affecting entire communities, historical developments, and the circumstances surrounding legendary individuals. Because heroic legends are intimately connected to the recorded achievements, failures, and destinies of their subjects, sufficiently significant alterations may propagate into the circumstances through which those legends are eventually remembered. Nevertheless, Amon does not simply possess the complete operation of parallel worlds or unrestricted control of every possible future. His domain concerns the errors through which an established or developing destiny may deviate from its intended course. When no legitimate point of deviation exists within the relationships accessible to him, the Sixth Magic cannot arbitrarily replace the outcome with an unrelated one.

Fate Siphoning — The Exchange of Predetermined Outcomes: Fate Siphoning is a specialized form of conceptual Theft through which Amon appropriates, redistributes, or exchanges the future circumstances associated with distinct individuals. Whereas Destiny Interference introduces discrepancies into the progression of events, Fate Siphoning directly affects the relationship between a person and the outcomes assigned to their existence. Amon may temporarily exchange the destinies of two individuals, appropriate another person’s fortunate future, transfer an approaching calamity to a different recipient, or substitute a comparatively insignificant future event for one whose consequences would otherwise prove catastrophic. The process depends upon establishing a viable relationship between the affected destinies, after which the Sixth Magic manifests an Error in their attribution. A fatal encounter intended for one individual may consequently become the fate of another, while the original recipient temporarily inherits the circumstances that should have belonged to the substitute. Such interference is exceptionally dangerous against opponents who rely upon predetermined outcomes, blessings of fortune, or prophecies guaranteeing particular developments. Nevertheless, Fate Siphoning is not equivalent to the unrestricted creation of favorable futures. The stolen destiny carries its own obligations and consequences, and any calamity appropriated by Amon becomes a genuine part of the continuity he must subsequently endure or redistribute. This is particularly significant when the stolen fate belongs to a Divine Spirit, Heroic Spirit, or being whose existence encompasses powerful spiritual obligations. Amon frequently distributes these burdens among his Segments, allowing the collective to absorb consequences that would otherwise compromise a single body. The technique also serves as a preliminary condition for more dangerous forms of Theft. By first stealing a target’s destiny and identity, Amon may create sufficient correspondence between himself and the target to appropriate supernatural characteristics that would ordinarily resist his interference.

Time Cheater — The Erroneous Flow of Time: Time Cheater is the temporal application of Worming Insanity, allowing Amon to manifest contradictions within the relationships governing the passage, measurement, and attribution of time. Unlike the Fifth Magic, whose deeper nature extends beyond its demonstrated temporal phenomena, Time Cheater does not possess unconditional dominion over time as an independent fundamental domain. Instead, it introduces errors into temporal processes, allowing particular objects, individuals, or regions to experience time differently from the surrounding World. Its most recognizable manifestation is an immense phantom clock of ancient stone, whose moving hands are composed of Amon’s Worms of Time. Through this phenomenon, Amon can accelerate, decelerate, suspend, or briefly reverse temporal progression within the effective region of his Magic. Objects exposed to accelerated time may undergo ten or twenty seconds of development during a single second of external observation, while those subjected to deceleration may appear almost motionless. Through selective designation, Amon can exclude particular individuals or objects from an affected temporal condition, permitting his Segments and chosen allies to act normally while the surrounding environment remains suspended. The Sixth Magic may also steal a specific temporal interval from a target, causing that interval to become unavailable to its original recipient and producing a discrepancy between the time experienced by the subject and the time recorded by the surrounding environment. More sophisticated applications allow Amon to interfere with brief causal sequences by temporarily reversing a localized event or recovering information from the recent history of an area. Such observation may manifest as a pale curtain of mist displaying recorded events, not through conventional clairvoyance, but by exploiting the persistent relationship between the present environment and the history responsible for producing it. These effects may extend across enormous areas when sufficiently powerful temporal correspondences are established, but they do not automatically constitute unrestricted revision of an entire timeline. Against Aoko Aozaki’s Fifth Magic, Amon would not necessarily prevail in a direct contest over temporal phenomena; nevertheless, he could interfere with the relationships through which a particular temporal alteration is attributed, recognized, or applied, potentially producing errors even in circumstances where he cannot overpower the Fifth Magic’s fundamental operation.

Worms of Time — The Subdivision of Temporal Identity: Worms of Time are manifestations of the Sixth Magic that express Amon’s identity as divisible spiritual characteristics capable of independent operation. They are neither ordinary familiars nor conventional fragments of a human soul, but portions of an existence whose individuality has been organized according to the principle of Error. A Worm of Time can serve as a carrier of Amon’s consciousness, a medium for Parasitism, an anchor for temporal interference, or the foundation through which another Segment may be established. Because Amon’s identity is not confined to the continuous existence of one physical organism, these fragments can maintain meaningful relationships with his main body even when separated by enormous distances. Each possesses a degree of spiritual correspondence sufficient to facilitate the transfer of information, stolen characteristics, or certain applications of Worming Insanity, depending upon the depth and completeness of the individual fragment. The Worms also function as the conceptual components of Time Cheater’s phantom clock, symbolizing the decomposition of temporal continuity into manipulable units. Amon can distribute these fragments among living beings, spiritual constructs, and carefully selected supernatural systems, transforming them into concealed points through which his Magic may subsequently manifest. The destruction of an individual Worm does not ordinarily endanger the totality of Amon’s existence, but its removal may eliminate a particular connection or interfere with a prepared application of Theft. At higher levels, the collective arrangement of Worms permits Amon to distribute the characteristics necessary for more powerful manifestations among multiple independent vessels, giving his spiritual structure a degree of modularity incompatible with the conventional organization of a singular living being.

Parasitism — The Theft of Life and Spiritual Occupation: Parasitism is the application of Theft to the relationship between a living organism and the spiritual existence inhabiting it. Through his Worms of Time, Amon can introduce fragments of his consciousness into a target’s body, soul, or spiritual framework, establishing himself as a parasitic participant in the host’s continued existence. The technique possesses two principal modes. In Concealed Mode, the parasite occupies a subordinate position within the host, perceiving the environment through the host’s senses while avoiding overt interference with its behavior. Because its access to the host’s consciousness remains incomplete, it may fail to recognize thoughts or communications occurring beyond the channels available to it. In Controlling Mode, the parasite establishes a deeper correspondence with the host’s spiritual structure, allowing Amon to appropriate control of the body, interpret thoughts, and influence the functions ordinarily belonging to the legitimate occupant. This condition is not simply possession by an external spirit, since Worming Insanity may cause the host’s own spiritual system to recognize the parasite as a valid participant in its existence. Amon can also employ Parasitism to extend the lifespan of an individual manifestation, recover from injuries, or infiltrate supernatural organizations without exposing his original body. However, the relationship between parasite and host is not entirely one-sided. The physical structure, instincts, spiritual attributes, and psychological condition of the host may influence the occupying fragment, particularly when its identity becomes deeply integrated with an unfamiliar organism. A parasite inhabiting an animal, for example, may gradually acquire instincts inconsistent with ordinary human cognition, creating a risk of identity deterioration unless it maintains connections with the greater Amon collective. Hosts possessing powerful spiritual protection, independent supernatural recognition, or appropriate ritual support may discover and expel the occupying entity. Religious Sacraments, specialized exorcism, and spiritual separation techniques may prove effective if they can isolate the parasite without triggering its defensive mechanisms. Parasitism can additionally extend to sufficiently complex Mystic Codes, artificial life forms, and spiritual constructs possessing characteristics analogous to life, allowing Amon to transform supernatural equipment into extensions of his distributed existence.

Segments (Avatars) — The Man Who May Be Everyone: Segments are autonomous manifestations of Amon created through the division, redistribution, or parasitic implantation of his spiritual characteristics. Although superficially comparable to familiars, artificial bodies, or divided spiritual manifestations, their underlying principle is fundamentally different. Each Segment possesses an authentic relationship with Amon’s identity, allowing it to function as a legitimate extension of the original rather than as a mere imitation. Through Worming Insanity, Amon has transformed individuality into a distributed metaphysical structure in which multiple independently acting existences may simultaneously possess a valid claim to being Amon. The scale of this phenomenon is extraordinary. Segments may inhabit human beings, animals, insects, microorganisms, and innumerable other suitable vessels, permitting his consciousness to spread throughout entire populations and environments. An apparent confrontation against a single Amon may therefore involve an immense network of concealed observers and potential manifestations, many of which possess sufficient independence to act without receiving immediate instructions from the original body. The characteristic monocle associated with Amon may manifest in altered forms corresponding to the anatomy of individual hosts, serving as a recurring indication of his identity. Segments can collect information, establish supernatural connections, perform Theft, manipulate destiny, or sacrifice themselves to produce advantageous causal disturbances. More powerful Segments may gather the characteristics of lesser ones and temporarily elevate their own status, while Depth 1 manifestations may be established through the separation of appropriate characteristics or the creation of an Error granting an incomplete vessel the recognized status of a more complete one. The destruction of Amon’s original body does not necessarily terminate his existence, since surviving Segments can preserve sufficient spiritual continuity to reconstruct an authentic Amon through their accumulated characteristics. This grants him an extraordinary form of distributed persistence, approaching practical omnipresence wherever his network has been established, without making him literally present throughout all existence. Moreover, because the identity of Amon can remain anchored through innumerable participating lives, his continued manifestation need not depend exclusively upon the environmental Mystery that supported his original Phantasmal Beast existence. Nevertheless, an individual Segment is not automatically equivalent to the complete original. Lesser manifestations lack his accumulated Mystery, possess more limited characteristics, and may remain vulnerable to Magecraft that would be ineffective against his original body. Only through sufficient redistribution or convergence can they approximate the spiritual completeness of Amon himself.

Synchronization — The Distributed Consciousness: Synchronization is the process through which Amon controls the exchange of information, memories, stolen characteristics, and supernatural conditions among his Segments. Because these manifestations are independently functioning extensions of a shared metaphysical identity, they do not require constant conscious communication to remain connected. Amon can selectively determine which information is transmitted, which memories remain isolated, and which Segments possess awareness of the activities undertaken by their counterparts. This selective distribution enables elaborate deception, since an individual Segment may genuinely believe itself to be the original Amon while possessing no knowledge of a larger operation. Such ignorance does not necessarily compromise the collective; it may instead prevent an enemy capable of extracting information from discovering the location or intentions of the main body. Synchronization also allows multiple Segments to coordinate the application of Theft against a single target. Since each Segment constitutes a valid manifestation of Amon’s identity, their simultaneous interference can increase the probability that at least one manifestation establishes the necessary correspondence for a successful appropriation. Against powerful Divine Spirits, Grand-class Servants, or other extraordinary existences, the accumulation of numerous independently established opportunities may prove more effective than concentrating all available power into a single direct attempt. Nevertheless, the limitations governing repeated Theft from an individual target are shared throughout the collective where the underlying stolen relationship remains singular. Synchronization cannot simply transform one valid opportunity into an unlimited number of independent possessions. Its principal advantage lies in allowing Amon to distribute the search for viable errors, the consequences of failed interference, and the spiritual burden of successful appropriation across an enormous network of interconnected manifestations.

Aging Cell — The Theft of Lifespan: Aging Cell is the manifestation of Worming Insanity within the relationship between a living being and its remaining duration of existence. By stealing the lifespan attributed to a target, Amon can shorten the period during which that being would ordinarily remain alive and appropriate the stolen duration for himself or another suitable recipient. The effect may manifest through rapid physical deterioration, accelerated senescence, and the appearance of age-related characteristics, but its fundamental nature is not ordinary temporal acceleration. Rather, the target loses a portion of its allotted survival, and the resulting physical changes reflect the spiritual discrepancy between its existing body and the reduced lifespan remaining to it. Consequently, stealing years from an infant does not necessarily produce a physically mature adult. The infant may instead exhibit the deterioration associated with advanced age while remaining developmentally immature. This distinction separates Aging Cell from the much more sophisticated Theft of developmental history, through which Amon may interfere with the experiences and stages belonging to a particular period of life. Such a Theft concerns the conceptual record of maturation rather than the mere quantity of remaining survival. Aging Cell is exceptionally dangerous against beings whose immortality depends upon avoiding conventional physical deterioration, although it does not automatically override an existence whose lifespan is undefined or whose fundamental nature is independent of aging. In situations where Amon retains the stolen temporal duration, he may subsequently return it, allowing the affected condition to reverse if the target’s spiritual and biological continuity remains recoverable. The technique therefore represents an appropriation of life as a finite existential relationship rather than simply an attack that inflicts the physical symptoms of old age.

Spiritual Foundation Theft — The Appropriation of Saint Graph Characteristics: Spiritual Foundation Theft is the application of Worming Insanity to the information constituting a Servant, Divine Spirit, or comparable spiritual existence. A Saint Graph is not merely a container of Magical Energy, but the structured foundation determining the manifested identity, abilities, spiritual characteristics, and existential parameters of the entity it supports. By identifying errors within the relationship between a Spiritual Foundation and its constituent characteristics, Amon may appropriate individual Skills, attributes, conceptual properties, or portions of a supernatural ability without necessarily destroying the entire foundation. In the case of a Heroic Spirit, the Theft of a characteristic associated with its legend may temporarily deprive the manifested Servant of that property while allowing Amon to exercise the stolen function through a compatible Segment or substituted identity. Noble Phantasms present a particularly complicated target because their operation may depend upon True Names, historical recognition, spiritual ownership, and conditions inherited from the original legend. Amon can interfere with these relationships, but appropriating the ability to activate a Noble Phantasm is not necessarily equivalent to possessing the original weapon or reproducing every aspect of its Mystery. By establishing a sufficiently sophisticated Error, however, the Sixth Magic can cause a stolen function to recognize Amon as an authorized user, potentially allowing the manifestation of effects that would otherwise be inaccessible to his spiritual structure. Against Grand-class Servants, whose Saint Graphs are substantially greater than those of conventional Servants, successful interference demands correspondingly more elaborate preparation, stronger connections, or the coordinated action of numerous Segments. The Sixth Magic does not automatically reduce such a being to an ordinary spiritual target, but neither does the scale of its Saint Graph make every constituent relationship inaccessible to Error. The decisive question is whether Amon can establish a legitimate contradiction in the spiritual recognition supporting the particular characteristic he intends to steal.

Authority Theft — The Usurpation of Divine Privilege: Authority Theft is among the most dangerous manifestations of Worming Insanity, permitting Amon to interfere with the metaphysical rights through which gods and other exceptional existences exercise powers beyond ordinary Magecraft. Within TYPE-MOON, a Divine Authority is not merely a supernatural technique requiring sufficient Magical Energy. It is the privilege through which an authorized existence realizes a particular result because it possesses the right to do so. Amon’s Sixth Magic approaches this principle through the contradiction between the legitimate bearer of an Authority and the recognition permitting that Authority to function. Rather than necessarily overpowering the Divine Spirit, he may attempt to steal the relationship by which the supernatural privilege is attributed to its owner. When successful, the corresponding Authority may cease to respond to its original bearer and instead recognize Amon, a Segment, or a substituted spiritual identity as its authorized possessor. Against a weakened Divine Spirit, this could permit the complete appropriation of a subordinate Authority or, under exceptionally favorable conditions, the larger divine privilege encompassing it. Such Theft may temporarily transfer the associated abilities, divine characteristics, and applicable supernatural permissions, making Amon capable of exercising powers belonging to phenomena fundamentally different from his original Phantasmal nature. Nevertheless, the appropriation of an Authority does not automatically transform Amon into the original god in every metaphysical respect, nor does it grant unlimited control over the divine system from which that Authority originated. Its successful operation may depend upon inherited obligations, the recognition of worshippers, spiritual compatibility, and the continued validity of the stolen relationship. Against a Beast of Humanity, the principal difficulty becomes still greater, since its existence may be sustained by extraordinary conceptual conditions, planetary relationships, and a spiritual structure far beyond ordinary Divine Spirits. Even a sufficiently weakened Beast may require extensive preparatory Theft, countless coordinated attempts, and the temporary appropriation of its destiny before a significant characteristic can be stolen. Against an Ultimate One, the distinction between an accessible manifestation and the fundamental nature of an extraterrestrial existence becomes especially important. Amon may interfere with characteristics that possess recognizable relationships within the affected environment, but he cannot automatically appropriate the totality of an alien existence merely because he can identify a trait associated with it. Authority Theft thus preserves the Sixth Magic’s capacity to threaten the highest classes of supernatural beings without treating every form of existence as interchangeable.

Texture Theft — The Appropriation of the World’s Surface: Texture Theft is an extreme manifestation of Worming Insanity that extends conceptual appropriation beyond individual objects and spiritual entities to the structured layers through which the World presents reality. A Texture is a metaphysical foundation supporting a particular arrangement of physical laws, supernatural principles, historical recognition, and the conditions under which existence is experienced. By establishing a sufficiently extensive relationship between a Texture and the system that recognizes its placement, Amon can attempt to steal its attribution, causing an enormous region of reality to become temporarily disconnected from the position or authority through which it is ordinarily maintained. The scale of this phenomenon may extend across an entire continent, making it one of the most outrageous applications of the Sixth Magic. Such an occurrence does not necessarily mean Amon physically compresses a continental landmass into his body or acquires permanent ownership of the planet. Rather, the Theft operates upon the metaphysical relationship through which the Texture is attached to its established location and recognized as the legitimate surface of reality in that region. Its temporary removal may produce extraordinary distortions in local phenomena, historical correspondence, supernatural systems, and the relationship between the affected surface and the underlying World. The absence of a suitable alternative foundation may force the stolen Texture to return to its original placement, since Amon’s Magic cannot indefinitely maintain an existence whose necessary relationships have been completely eliminated. Nevertheless, even a momentary displacement demonstrates the Sixth Magic’s capacity to interfere with structures far exceeding the scale of ordinary thaumaturgy. Unlike Marble Phantasm or Reality Marble, which manifest phenomena according to their respective mechanisms, Texture Theft does not necessarily generate a new world or overwrite the existing one. It appropriates the recognized placement of an existing layer of reality through an Error in its relationship with the World. The distinction is crucial because it allows the phenomenon to possess continental or potentially greater consequences without granting Amon unrestricted authorship of planetary laws.

Theft of Anchors — The Severance of Existential Recognition: Anchor Theft is the ability to appropriate the connections that maintain an existence within a particular supernatural system. In TYPE-MOON, many spiritual beings depend upon external relationships to sustain their manifestation, whether through accumulated faith, collective recognition, contracts, summoning foundations, or the Magical Energy supplied by an external source. Amon can identify these relationships and manifest Errors that transfer, interrupt, or sever the recognition through which they operate. Against a conventional magus, the technique may deprive the target of access to a contracted source of energy or interfere with the connection sustaining a familiar. Against a Servant, it may disrupt the relationship between Master and Servant, potentially preventing the normal transfer of Magical Energy without necessarily destroying the Servant’s independent spiritual foundation. Against a Divine Spirit, Anchor Theft becomes considerably more dangerous, since the stolen relationship may involve worship, divine recognition, or the historical conditions maintaining a particular manifestation. By appropriating sufficient anchors, Amon can temporarily weaken the stability of an otherwise superior spiritual existence, increasing the effectiveness of subsequent Theft against its characteristics or Authorities. The technique may also permit Amon to acquire some of the benefits ordinarily conferred by those anchors, though this does not automatically grant him the complete existential status of their original recipient. Anchor Theft is especially significant during rituals involving spiritual elevation, divine manifestation, or the transformation of a being into a higher supernatural state, since the sudden loss of a supporting relationship may destabilize the process before its completion. Its ultimate danger is that it does not need to destroy the power possessed by an opponent. It may instead deprive that opponent of the relationship necessary to access or sustain it.

Conceptual Theft — Distance, Convergence, and Abstract Properties: Conceptual Theft is the application of Worming Insanity to properties that cannot be meaningfully represented as physical possessions. At Depth 0, Amon may appropriate such things as distance, temporal intervals, the convergence of separate phenomena, or other abstract relationships, provided an admissible foundation for Theft exists. The Theft of distance, for example, does not require conventional movement through the space separating two locations. Instead, Amon interferes with the relationship establishing that separation, temporarily depriving the interval of its recognized spatial significance. Within the limits of his perception and the established connection between the affected positions, this may allow an apparently instantaneous transition without reproducing the operation of the Second Magic. Similarly, stealing the convergence of multiple phenomena may interfere with the condition through which those events would otherwise meet, combine, or produce a shared consequence. This can disrupt coordinated attacks, supernatural rituals, or conceptual phenomena whose operation depends upon the intersection of multiple conditions. Amon may also appropriate temporal categories such as day and night when they possess recognizable significance within a particular region or Texture. The resulting disturbance is not necessarily an alteration of astronomical motion, but an Error in the attribution of the temporal conditions through which the affected World recognizes those states. If both categories become simultaneously inaccessible, the region may enter an abnormal condition in which ordinarily distinct temporal principles cannot be reconciled. Such phenomena demonstrate the absurd conceptual reach of Worming Insanity, since even abstract relationships can become objects of Theft once they possess a distinguishable existence within the system being affected. Nevertheless, the Sixth Magic does not treat every abstract proposition as an independent possession. The target must constitute a recognizable property or relationship whose attribution can meaningfully be made erroneous.

Law of Similarity — Collective Error Propagation: Law of Similarity is an application through which Worming Insanity extends a single established Error across multiple subjects sharing an appropriate conceptual or spiritual correspondence. Similarity has long served as an important principle within sympathetic and ritualistic Magecraft, allowing particular phenomena to operate through the relationship between an object and its representation. Amon’s Sixth Magic elevates this principle beyond conventional thaumaturgical application by introducing errors into the conditions through which similarity establishes supernatural correspondence. If multiple beings possess a shared characteristic, spiritual classification, contractual relationship, or sufficiently significant connection, Amon may cause an Error affecting one subject to propagate among other members of the corresponding group. The affected individuals do not necessarily need to occupy the same physical location, since the relevant relationship may exist at the spiritual or conceptual level. This makes the technique especially dangerous against organizations whose members share common foundations, Servants summoned through related systems, or groups whose supernatural protection depends upon the same governing principle. Even a Grand-class Servant may become vulnerable to a carefully established group-wide Error if its relevant characteristics possess sufficient correspondence with those of the original target. However, the existence of a broad classification does not automatically make every member equally susceptible. The Sixth Magic must establish that the particular relationship being exploited is genuinely shared by the affected subjects. The more independent and internally complete the spiritual identities involved, the more difficult it becomes to propagate the Error without additional preparatory conditions. Nevertheless, successful collective propagation allows a single conceptual contradiction to produce consequences across an enormous number of targets, making the technique disproportionately dangerous when combined with Amon’s distributed network of Segments.

Substitution and Separation — The Reassignment of Presence: Substitution and Separation are defensive applications of Worming Insanity that manipulate the recognition of Amon’s location, presence, and relationship with the surrounding World. Through Substitution, Amon can cause another suitable manifestation to occupy the causal or conceptual position ordinarily assigned to his original body. The substitute may be a Segment, shadow, spiritual fragment, or another object possessing sufficient correspondence with him. An attack may consequently strike the apparently correct target while its effects are transferred elsewhere, or the destruction of an apparent body may fail to affect the authentic continuity of Amon’s existence. Separation operates through a different principle, creating an Error between Amon’s existence and the conditions by which the surrounding World recognizes interaction with him. While this condition persists, attacks originating within the affected reality may fail to establish a valid relationship with his body, rendering him effectively inaccessible to ordinary interference. However, the separation is reciprocal. An Amon who has excluded himself from the interaction conditions of the surrounding World cannot freely affect that same environment without establishing another connection or terminating the Error. This distinguishes the technique from unrestricted intangibility. Its defensive value is extraordinary, but it remains dependent upon the stability of the contradiction through which the separation is maintained. Against powerful conceptual attacks, Amon may prefer to combine Substitution with Segments, allowing the consequences of hostile interference to be distributed across multiple independent manifestations. This combination makes the question of whether an opponent has genuinely reached Amon substantially more difficult than merely identifying his physical location.

Dream Traversal — The Passage Through Imagined Relations: Dream Traversal is an unconventional application of Loophole through which Amon utilizes the relationships between consciousness, dreams, and spiritual experience as a medium of movement or infiltration. Within TYPE-MOON, dreams may possess genuine supernatural significance and can serve as environments through which spiritual information, memories, or connections are experienced. Amon exploits the distinction between an imagined presence and a spiritually meaningful manifestation, introducing an Error through which the former may function as the latter. By establishing a connection to a suitable dream, he can enter the corresponding spiritual environment, travel through relationships maintained by dreaming consciousnesses, or use the dream as an intermediary point for the manifestation of a Segment. The technique does not simply grant unrestricted teleportation to any location imagined by an individual. Its effectiveness depends upon the existence of a recognizable connection between the consciousness, its spiritual foundation, and the destination or subject represented within the dream. At greater depth, Dream Traversal may also allow Amon to exploit the expiration of an imagined or temporary phenomenon, causing his own presence to be recognized as part of the disappearing construct and thereby escaping a restricted environment. This form of departure is particularly difficult to prevent with ordinary spatial confinement, since the Error concerns the relationship between the entity and the phenomenon through which it is recognized rather than a conventional path through physical space.

Regression to the Age of Gods — The Mystery of the Original Body: Regression to the Age of Gods is an innate characteristic of Amon’s Phantasmal Beast physiology rather than an application granted exclusively by the Sixth Magic. As an existence originating from an era in which Mystery was substantially greater than in modern human civilization, Amon possesses an ancient spiritual constitution capable of reproducing certain supernatural principles associated with the Age of Gods. His accumulated Mystery, exceeding three millennia, grants his original body exceptional resistance against ordinary modern Magecraft. In encounters between Mysteries of vastly different depth, the older and more powerful Mystery may overwhelm the weaker one before its intended effect can meaningfully manifest. Consequently, contemporary thaumaturgical attacks that would threaten ordinary humans or magi may prove ineffective against Amon even when he does not actively employ Worming Insanity. This resistance should not be confused with unconditional immunity to supernatural interference. Divine Constructs, powerful ancient Mysteries, exceptional primordial techniques, and other True Magics may possess sufficient mystery or appropriate conceptual properties to affect him directly. Furthermore, the protection conferred by his original Phantasmal nature does not automatically extend to every Segment. Lesser manifestations may lack the depth of Mystery necessary to disregard modern Magecraft, allowing skilled opponents to identify and destroy them even when confronting the original remains extraordinarily difficult. Regression to the Age of Gods therefore provides Amon with an independent foundation of supernatural resilience, while Worming Insanity supplies the mechanisms through which he can further evade, redirect, or invalidate hostile phenomena.

Interaction with the Counter Force — The Error the World Cannot Easily Correct: Worming Insanity presents an exceptional problem for the Counter Force because its phenomena do not necessarily appear as straightforward violations imposed upon the World from outside. The Counter Force acts to preserve the continued existence of the World and humanity by responding to threats capable of endangering those objectives. Amon’s Sixth Magic, however, frequently operates through relationships that the affected system already recognizes, using admissible contradictions to produce outcomes that would ordinarily be impossible. As a result, the immediate manifestation of an Error may not resemble the direct destruction of a law or the forcible replacement of an existing Texture. The affected phenomenon may continue operating according to its established principles while simultaneously producing an erroneous result. This does not place Amon beyond the Counter Force’s jurisdiction. Should his activities threaten the continued existence of humanity or the planet, an appropriate corrective response may still occur, potentially employing agents, circumstances, or supernatural forces capable of severing the relationships supporting his interference. Amon’s advantage lies in his ability to distribute the consequences of such responses across Segments, manipulate the recognition of their intended targets, and exploit the causal circumstances through which corrective actions are executed. His widespread spiritual network also complicates attempts to isolate him as a singular threat, since the destruction of one manifestation may not eliminate the independent continuities preserving his existence elsewhere. Nevertheless, the World is not required to tolerate every error indefinitely. Certain contradictions may collapse when their supporting conditions disappear, while sufficiently powerful corrective phenomena may eliminate an established relationship before Amon can exploit it. The Sixth Magic therefore creates an extraordinary contest between the mechanisms through which the World preserves continuity and the principle through which continuity can be made erroneous, without establishing that either side is unconditionally superior.

Ultimate Limitation — The Impossibility of an Impossible Error: Despite its extraordinary conceptual reach, Worming Insanity possesses a fundamental limitation: it cannot actualize an error that has no possible foundation for existence. Amon may identify and manifest contradictions within the relationships governing phenomena, but he cannot simply invent an entirely unrelated possibility and compel reality to accept it without an appropriate basis. Similarity, established connections, incomplete rules, contradictory recognition, or an environment capable of supporting erroneous interpretation must provide a viable foundation for the Magic’s operation. This restriction distinguishes Worming Insanity from omnipotence and establishes the difference between an existence that actualizes errors and one that possesses unrestricted authority to create reality. Its extraordinary scale does not diminish this limitation. Amon may interfere with the recognized ownership of Divine Authorities, the characteristics of Grand-class Saint Graphs, the fate of historical individuals, or even the attribution of a continental Texture, but each application must still satisfy the conditions through which the relevant contradiction can occur. Moreover, successful Theft does not necessarily guarantee the capacity to retain or exercise everything appropriated. An overwhelmingly powerful spiritual characteristic may require redistribution among Segments, prolonged preparation, or the theft of supporting relationships before it can be safely accommodated. A stolen destiny may contain catastrophic obligations, while the appropriation of an incompatible identity may threaten the coherence of Amon’s own existence. Certain entities may also possess forms of metaphysical completeness or independence that provide no accessible contradiction within the domain under examination. The Swirl of the Root itself remains beyond the jurisdiction of Worming Insanity, since the undifferentiated source of all possible phenomena cannot be treated as an ordinary structured system possessing a separable error to exploit. Finally, Amon’s own personality constitutes a limitation distinct from the mechanisms of his Magic. Despite his willingness to endanger countless Segments, he is fundamentally unwilling to accept a genuine possibility of his total annihilation. An opponent capable of threatening the continuity of his entire distributed existence may therefore force him to abandon a confrontation that he could otherwise continue indefinitely. This does not render his Magic weak; rather, it establishes that possessing an extraordinary means of avoiding defeat does not necessarily grant the psychological willingness to accept an unavoidable risk.
